<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-26" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - 4-momentum</title>


  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let's consider the creation of the polyelectron as described in the text.
            How do we find the photon energy required to create the electron-positron
            pair?
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We are looking for the <alert>minimum</alert> energy to create
                        the pair.
                        In the lab frame, not all of the energy is available for
                        creation of the pair.
                        In what frame is total energy = available energy?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Draw a before-after picture of the collision in the frame of
                        part a).
                        What does this tell you about the magnitude of the system
                        4-momentum?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now draw the 4-momenta of the particles in the lab frame.
                        Use the known magnitude of the system 4-momentum to find the
                        energy of the photon.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                The total electrical energy generated on Earth in 2025 was roughly
                31000 terawatt-hours.
                To how much mass is this energy equivalent?
                In the actual production of this electrical energy, is this much
                mass converted to energy, more, or less?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Elite cyclists can output about 450W of power while pedaling, but
                the efficiency of the human body is about 25%, so this useful work
                is only a quarter of the total power output.
                At this rate, how long will it take for a cyclist to convert 1kg of
                mass into energy?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                How do people ever lose weight?
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        In a circular particle collider (like the LHC), two particles of mass
        <m>
            m
        </m>
        and total energy
        <m>
            E
        </m>
        and are shot toward one another and collide head-on, so the system 4-momentum is
        <m>
            (2E,0)
        </m>
        . This means that the energy
        <m>
            2E
        </m>
        is available to be converted to whatever form. In a linear particle accelerator, a particle of mass
        <m>
            m
        </m>
        and energy
        <m>
            E'
        </m>
        hits a fixed target of mass
        <m>
            m
        </m>
        . How large should
        <m>
            E'
        </m>
        be so that the collision is equivalent to the one in the circular collider?
    </p>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
